# ==============================================================================
# .netrc Template — Setup Instructions
# ==============================================================================
#
# READ ALL OF THIS BEFORE YOU TOUCH THE FILE. The Python netrc parser is
# finicky and three things have historically broken setup. They are all easy
# to avoid if you follow the steps below in order.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 1 — Create the file at the right location AS A FILE (not a folder)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# On Windows, save the file as:
#     C:\Users\<YourUsername>\.netrc
#
# On macOS / Linux, save it as:
#     ~/.netrc
#
# Either `_netrc` or `.netrc` works on Windows. We recommend `.netrc` because
# it's portable across all platforms. Whichever you pick, IT MUST BE A FILE.
# If you ever see Get-Content / Python report "permission denied" or "is a
# directory", you accidentally created a folder named `.netrc`. Delete it and
# create the file properly.
#
# Quick PowerShell sanity check:
#     Get-Item ~/.netrc | Select-Object Attributes
# Should print "Archive". If it prints "Directory", fix it before continuing.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 2 — Copy ONLY the credential block below into your real .netrc
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# DO NOT copy any of these comment lines. Python's `netrc` module accepts the
# `#` comment syntax in theory, but in practice the stdlib parser has a bug
# where lengthy comment blocks before a `machine` entry produce cryptic errors
# like:
#
#     netrc.NetrcParseError: bad toplevel token '---' (line N)
#
# The error message often points to the wrong line. The safe move is to
# include only machine/login/password lines and nothing else.
#
# A valid .netrc looks like this (each machine on its own block):
#
# ------------------------------- BEGIN EXAMPLE -------------------------------
# machine urs.earthdata.nasa.gov
#   login YOUR_EARTHDATA_USERNAME
#   password YOUR_EARTHDATA_PASSWORD
# machine dataspace.copernicus.eu
#   login YOUR_COPERNICUS_USERNAME
#   password YOUR_COPERNICUS_PASSWORD
# machine ers.cr.usgs.gov
#   login YOUR_USGS_USERNAME
#   password YOUR_USGS_PASSWORD
# -------------------------------- END EXAMPLE --------------------------------
#
# Two-space indentation on `login`/`password` is optional but conventional.
# Leading whitespace is fine. Trailing comments on the same line are NOT —
# remove them.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 3 — Secure the file
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# macOS / Linux:
#     chmod 600 ~/.netrc
#
# Windows: the default user ACL is fine. Do NOT remove your own user from the
# ACL; if you ever see your scripts report "Access denied" on the .netrc, run:
#     icacls "$env:USERPROFILE\.netrc" /grant "${env:USERNAME}:(R)"
#
# Verify Python can read it (this is what every script actually uses):
#     python -c "import netrc, os; print(netrc.netrc(os.path.expanduser('~/.netrc')).hosts.keys())"
# You should see a set containing 'urs.earthdata.nasa.gov' (at minimum).
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 4 — One-time HyP3 application approval (Earthdata only)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Even with valid credentials, NASA Earthdata requires you to authorize each
# downstream application against your account ONCE per account, in a browser.
# The first time you call `hyp3_sdk.HyP3()` you'll see:
#
#     AuthenticationError: Pre authorization required for this application...
#
# Open this URL while logged into your Earthdata account and click APPROVE:
#     https://urs.earthdata.nasa.gov/approve_app?client_id=BO_n7nTIlMljdvU6kRRB3g
#
# This is one-time per Earthdata account. After approval, every future SDK
# call authenticates silently.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Account signup URLs (sign up first, then come back to fill credentials)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   NASA Earthdata:                https://urs.earthdata.nasa.gov/
#   Copernicus Data Space (CDSE):  https://dataspace.copernicus.eu/
#   USGS EarthExplorer:            https://earthexplorer.usgs.gov/
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CRITICAL — NEVER commit your real .netrc / _netrc
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The project .gitignore blocks these files, but double-check before pushing.
# ==============================================================================
#
# ---  template body — REMOVE EVERYTHING ABOVE THIS LINE when you save your real .netrc  ---

machine urs.earthdata.nasa.gov
  login YOUR_EARTHDATA_USERNAME
  password YOUR_EARTHDATA_PASSWORD
machine dataspace.copernicus.eu
  login YOUR_COPERNICUS_USERNAME
  password YOUR_COPERNICUS_PASSWORD
machine ers.cr.usgs.gov
  login YOUR_USGS_USERNAME
  password YOUR_USGS_PASSWORD
